            0.111,
            0.135,
            0.678,
            0.156
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.6.1. Kingdom Monera — Unicellular prokaryotes"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.164,
            0.401,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 12.4: Let us explore",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.185,
            0.775,
            0.22
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. In the school laboratory, observe the available permanent slides of bacteria and cyanobacteria under the microscope."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.222,
            0.414,
            0.24
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Compare them with Fig. 12.6."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.243,
            0.766,
            0.414
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you observe? Bacteria and cyanobacteria are single-celled prokaryotes that are grouped under Monera (Fig. 12.6). Bacteria are found everywhere, including soil, water, air, hot springs and other extreme environments where most organisms cannot survive and they are present even inside the human bodies. They are also found in the gut of ruminants and are responsible for the production of biogas from the dung of these animals. Some bacteria are harmful (pathogens) and cause diseases but many are useful like Lactobacillus and Rhizobium. Cyanobacteria are autotrophs and decomposers. In addition to nutrient cycling, some bacteria also break down pollutants like oil, pesticides, sewage, and so on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.09,
            0.417,
            0.166,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.09,
                0.417,
                0.166,
                0.47
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.427,
            0.465,
            0.445
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Bridging Science and Society"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.46,
            0.228,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.126,
                0.46,
                0.228,
                0.555
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.232,
            0.453,
            0.733,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ram Bux Singh is popularly known as the ‘Father of Modern Biogas’. He was a pioneering Indian scientist who contributed to the development of biogas technology. In 1957, he established India’s first scientifically designed biogas plant at Ramnagar, Sitapur, Uttar Pradesh, which marked the beginning of systematic modern biogas research in the country. He later worked extensively on developing low-cost,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.56,
            0.732,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "efficient biogas plants for rural areas. He also served as an international consultant and helped set up biogas plants in several countries. His lifelong efforts played a crucial role in promoting renewable energy, waste management and sustainable development through technology."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.653,
            0.663,
            0.672
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.6.2 Kingdom Protista — Unicellular eukaryotes"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.675,
            0.766,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "All single-celled eukaryotes without cell wall or with cell wall made up of cellulose are grouped under the kingdom protista. They are microscopic and highly diverse organisms. You can study some of these protists (Fig. 12.7) by making a slide from hay infusion as explained below."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.752,
            0.378,
            0.77
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 12.5: Let us make"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.773,
            0.606,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To make a hay infusion, follow the steps given below —"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.792,
            0.67,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Collect a small sample of grass after the lawn has been mowed, or collect straw or fodder."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.827,
            0.67,
            0.861
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Take a small glass bottle and fill one fourth of it with the grass, straw or fodder."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.681,
            0.77,
            0.769,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 20,
              "bbox": [
                0.681,
                0.77,
                0.769,
                0.825
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_20.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.861,
            0.671,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Fill the bottle with stagnant water or pond water and mix it with the collected plant material."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.896,
            0.691,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Cover the bottle with a muslin cloth and tie it using a thread."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.784,
            0.108,
            0.947,
            0.206
          ],
          "content": [
