        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 29,
          "bbox": [
            0.839,
            0.25,
            0.956,
            0.287
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 30,
          "bbox": [
            0.789,
            0.294,
            0.95,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cyanobacteria (blue-green algae) were among the first organisms to produce oxygen through photosynthesis. About 2.5 billion years ago, oxygen accumulated in the atmosphere, made the Earth suitable for other forms of life. Fossils of ancient cyanobacteria are found in structures called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "stromatolites",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". They were found in Rajasthan and Madhya Pradesh. They provide some of the earliest evidence of life on the Earth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
